1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Welcome to School Physics, Explained.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:08,384
This is High School Physics, Unit 3,
Forces and Newton's Laws, Part 1. In this

3
00:00:08,384 --> 00:00:10,647
video, we'll cover four topics.

4
00:00:11,097 --> 00:00:12,249
What Is a Force?.

5
00:00:12,699 --> 00:00:14,534
Newton's First Law and Inertia.

6
00:00:14,984 --> 00:00:16,371
Newton's Second Law.

7
00:00:16,821 --> 00:00:18,762
Newton's Third Law: Force Pairs.

8
00:00:19,212 --> 00:00:20,428
Let's get started.

9
00:00:22,066 --> 00:00:27,273
Kick a ball, pull a door, or drop your
keys. Something always makes the motion

10
00:00:27,273 --> 00:00:27,741
change.

11
00:00:28,191 --> 00:00:30,260
In physics, that something is a force.

12
00:00:31,410 --> 00:00:33,927
A force is a push or a pull on an object.

13
00:00:34,377 --> 00:00:40,033
Every force has a size and a direction, so
a force is a vector. We draw it as an

14
00:00:40,033 --> 00:00:40,457
arrow.

15
00:00:40,907 --> 00:00:45,921
We measure force in newtons. One newton is
about the weight of a small apple.

16
00:00:47,071 --> 00:00:50,377
Some forces need contact. The objects have
to touch.

17
00:00:50,827 --> 00:00:56,569
A hand pushing a box is an applied force.
A floor pushing up on the box is the

18
00:00:56,569 --> 00:00:57,526
normal force.

19
00:00:57,976 --> 00:01:02,680
Friction acts along the surface and
resists sliding. And a rope pulling on

20
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:04,205
something gives tension.

21
00:01:05,355 --> 00:01:10,945
Other forces act at a distance, with no
contact at all. We call them field forces.

22
00:01:11,395 --> 00:01:15,789
Gravity pulls a falling apple toward the
Earth, even through empty air.

23
00:01:16,239 --> 00:01:20,826
Magnets and electric charges can also push
or pull without touching.

24
00:01:21,976 --> 00:01:25,304
Usually several forces act on an object at
once.

25
00:01:25,754 --> 00:01:30,482
A book resting on a table has gravity
pulling it down, and the normal force

26
00:01:30,482 --> 00:01:31,365
pushing it up.

27
00:01:31,815 --> 00:01:37,275
The two forces are equal in size and
opposite in direction, so they cancel. The

28
00:01:37,275 --> 00:01:40,177
net force is zero, and the book stays put.

29
00:01:41,327 --> 00:01:44,101
When the forces don't cancel, there's a
net force.

30
00:01:44,551 --> 00:01:49,073
Pull a sled forward with thirty newtons
while friction pulls back with ten.

31
00:01:49,523 --> 00:01:55,081
The net force is twenty newtons forward.
That unbalanced force is what changes the

32
00:01:55,081 --> 00:01:56,030
sled's motion.

33
00:01:57,180 --> 00:01:58,652
Now try one yourself.

34
00:01:59,102 --> 00:02:04,495
Two people push a crate. One pushes right
with forty newtons, the other pushes left

35
00:02:04,495 --> 00:02:07,614
with twenty five newtons. What is the net
force?

36
00:02:08,064 --> 00:02:09,344
Pause the video now.

37
00:02:09,794 --> 00:02:15,410
The forces point in opposite directions,
so subtract. Forty minus twenty five is

38
00:02:15,410 --> 00:02:17,517
fifteen newtons, to the right.

39
00:02:17,817 --> 00:02:22,745
Slide a hockey puck across the ice, and it
glides a long way before it stops.

40
00:02:23,195 --> 00:02:26,886
On a perfectly frictionless surface, would
it ever stop at all?

41
00:02:28,036 --> 00:02:32,580
For centuries, people thought moving
things naturally slow down and stop.

42
00:02:33,030 --> 00:02:38,303
Galileo and Newton saw it differently.
Things slow down because a force, like

43
00:02:38,303 --> 00:02:39,878
friction, acts on them.

44
00:02:40,328 --> 00:02:43,869
Take friction away, and a moving object
just keeps going.

45
00:02:45,019 --> 00:02:46,640
That's Newton's first law.

46
00:02:47,090 --> 00:02:52,365
An object at rest stays at rest, and an
object in motion keeps moving at a

47
00:02:52,365 --> 00:02:55,858
constant velocity, unless a net force acts
on it.

48
00:02:56,308 --> 00:02:59,978
Constant velocity means the same speed, in
a straight line.

49
00:03:01,128 --> 00:03:05,288
The tendency to keep doing what you're
already doing is called inertia.

50
00:03:05,738 --> 00:03:08,959
The more mass an object has, the more
inertia it has.

51
00:03:09,409 --> 00:03:14,145
A bowling ball is much harder to start,
stop, or turn than a tennis ball.

52
00:03:15,295 --> 00:03:16,959
You feel inertia in a car.

53
00:03:17,409 --> 00:03:22,121
When the car brakes suddenly, your body
keeps moving forward. The seat belt

54
00:03:22,121 --> 00:03:24,257
supplies the force that stops you.

55
00:03:24,707 --> 00:03:29,510
When the car pulls away quickly, you feel
pressed back into the seat, because your

56
00:03:29,510 --> 00:03:31,384
body tends to stay where it was.

57
00:03:32,534 --> 00:03:36,694
Here's the key point. Zero net force does
not mean zero motion.

58
00:03:37,144 --> 00:03:42,155
A car cruising at a steady sixty
kilometers per hour on a straight road has

59
00:03:42,155 --> 00:03:43,224
balanced forces.

60
00:03:43,674 --> 00:03:49,267
The engine's push forward exactly cancels
the drag and friction pulling back. The

61
00:03:49,267 --> 00:03:52,720
net force is zero, so the velocity doesn't
change.

62
00:03:53,870 --> 00:03:55,342
Now try one yourself.

63
00:03:55,792 --> 00:04:01,372
A spacecraft far from any star or planet
is coasting at two thousand meters per

64
00:04:01,372 --> 00:04:05,328
second, with its engines off. What happens
to its speed?

65
00:04:05,778 --> 00:04:07,058
Pause the video now.

66
00:04:07,508 --> 00:04:12,756
With no net force acting on it, it keeps
moving at two thousand meters per second,

67
00:04:12,756 --> 00:04:14,548
in a straight line, forever.

68
00:04:14,848 --> 00:04:18,133
Push an empty shopping cart, and it speeds
up easily.

69
00:04:18,583 --> 00:04:21,975
Push a full one just as hard, and it
barely gets going.

70
00:04:22,425 --> 00:04:27,529
Newton's second law tells us exactly how
force, mass and acceleration are

71
00:04:27,529 --> 00:04:28,228
connected.

72
00:04:29,378 --> 00:04:33,901
Here's a cart on a smooth, level track, so
we can ignore friction for now.

73
00:04:34,351 --> 00:04:38,745
We give it a steady push, and watch how
quickly its velocity changes.

74
00:04:39,195 --> 00:04:41,542
That rate of change is the acceleration.

75
00:04:42,692 --> 00:04:48,246
Start with a push of ten newtons on a two
kilogram cart. It accelerates at five

76
00:04:48,246 --> 00:04:50,073
meters per second squared.

77
00:04:50,523 --> 00:04:54,022
Now double the force to twenty newtons, on
the same cart.

78
00:04:54,472 --> 00:04:58,568
The acceleration doubles too, to ten
meters per second squared.

79
00:04:59,018 --> 00:05:01,898
Acceleration is proportional to the net
force.

80
00:05:03,048 --> 00:05:07,869
Next, keep the force at ten newtons, but
double the mass to four kilograms.

81
00:05:08,319 --> 00:05:13,375
Now the acceleration is cut in half, to
two and a half meters per second squared.

82
00:05:13,825 --> 00:05:16,940
Acceleration is inversely proportional to
mass.

83
00:05:18,090 --> 00:05:20,991
Put those together, and you get Newton's
second law.

84
00:05:21,441 --> 00:05:24,769
Acceleration equals net force divided by
mass.

85
00:05:25,219 --> 00:05:30,147
Or, as it's usually written, net force
equals mass times acceleration.

86
00:05:30,597 --> 00:05:36,059
One newton is the force that gives one
kilogram an acceleration of one meter per

87
00:05:36,059 --> 00:05:37,083
second squared.

88
00:05:38,233 --> 00:05:42,701
The word net matters. It means the sum of
all the forces, including their

89
00:05:42,701 --> 00:05:43,374
directions.

90
00:05:43,824 --> 00:05:48,717
Suppose you push a box with fifty newtons
to the right, while friction pulls back

91
00:05:48,717 --> 00:05:49,925
with twenty newtons.

92
00:05:50,375 --> 00:05:52,914
The net force is thirty newtons, to the
right.

93
00:05:53,364 --> 00:05:57,439
And the acceleration always points the
same way as the net force.

94
00:05:58,589 --> 00:06:00,253
Let's try a real example.

95
00:06:00,703 --> 00:06:06,164
A twelve hundred kilogram car has a net
force of three thousand newtons pushing it

96
00:06:06,164 --> 00:06:06,697
forward.

97
00:06:07,147 --> 00:06:12,189
Divide three thousand by twelve hundred,
and the acceleration is two and a half

98
00:06:12,189 --> 00:06:14,422
meters per second squared, forward.

99
00:06:15,572 --> 00:06:17,044
Now try one yourself.

100
00:06:17,494 --> 00:06:23,143
A five kilogram box is pushed with thirty
newtons, and friction pushes back with ten

101
00:06:23,143 --> 00:06:25,430
newtons. What is its acceleration?

102
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:27,160
Pause the video now.

103
00:06:27,610 --> 00:06:32,963
The net force is twenty newtons, so the
acceleration is twenty divided by five,

104
00:06:32,963 --> 00:06:35,674
which is four meters per second squared.

105
00:06:35,974 --> 00:06:41,030
Push on a wall, and the wall pushes back
on you. Lean on it, and you don't fall

106
00:06:41,030 --> 00:06:41,350
over.

107
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:44,936
Forces never come alone. They always come
in pairs.

108
00:06:46,086 --> 00:06:52,198
Newton's third law says: when object A
pushes on object B, object B pushes back

109
00:06:52,198 --> 00:06:53,126
on object A.

110
00:06:53,576 --> 00:06:57,480
The two forces are equal in size and
opposite in direction.

111
00:06:57,930 --> 00:07:02,005
We write it as F on B by A equals minus F
on A by B.

112
00:07:03,155 --> 00:07:05,352
To spot a force pair, swap the names.

113
00:07:05,802 --> 00:07:10,731
The Earth pulls down on you. So you pull
up on the Earth, with exactly the same

114
00:07:10,731 --> 00:07:11,605
size of force.

115
00:07:12,055 --> 00:07:16,743
Your feet push back on the ground when you
walk, and the ground pushes you forward.

116
00:07:16,743 --> 00:07:17,985
That's what moves you.

117
00:07:19,135 --> 00:07:22,783
If the forces are equal and opposite, why
don't they cancel?

118
00:07:23,233 --> 00:07:28,631
Because they act on different objects. One
force acts on A, the other acts on B.

119
00:07:29,081 --> 00:07:32,366
Forces only cancel when they act on the
same object.

120
00:07:33,516 --> 00:07:36,524
Here's a classic mix up. A book rests on a
table.

121
00:07:36,974 --> 00:07:42,311
Gravity pulls the book down, and the table
pushes the book up. These are equal, but

122
00:07:42,311 --> 00:07:46,169
they are not a third law pair, because
both act on the book.

123
00:07:46,619 --> 00:07:51,305
The real partner of the table's push is
the book pushing down on the table. And

124
00:07:51,305 --> 00:07:55,813
the partner of Earth's pull on the book is
the book pulling up on the Earth.

125
00:07:56,963 --> 00:08:00,099
Equal forces don't mean equal
accelerations.

126
00:08:00,549 --> 00:08:05,655
When a bug hits a car's windshield, the
bug and the car feel the same size of

127
00:08:05,655 --> 00:08:06,053
force.

128
00:08:06,503 --> 00:08:12,140
But the car has a huge mass, so its
acceleration is tiny. The bug's mass is

129
00:08:12,140 --> 00:08:14,695
tiny, so its acceleration is huge.

130
00:08:15,845 --> 00:08:17,317
Now try one yourself.

131
00:08:17,767 --> 00:08:22,827
A skater pushes off a wall with eighty
newtons. How big is the force the wall

132
00:08:22,827 --> 00:08:25,981
puts on the skater, and which way does it
point?

133
00:08:26,431 --> 00:08:27,711
Pause the video now.

134
00:08:28,161 --> 00:08:33,073
The wall pushes back on the skater with
eighty newtons, pointing away from the

135
00:08:33,073 --> 00:08:36,033
wall. That's what sends the skater gliding
off.

136
00:08:36,483 --> 00:08:42,262
Next time, we'll draw all the forces on an
object in one clear picture: free body

137
00:08:42,262 --> 00:08:42,904
diagrams.

138
00:08:44,054 --> 00:08:48,889
If this lesson helped, subscribe to School
Physics, Explained, and tap the bell so

139
00:08:48,889 --> 00:08:50,539
you don't miss the next one.

